  "content": "<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><div id=\"\"> <p>APOD</p> <p>Astronomy Picture of the Day</p> <p> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer. </p> <div><p><img width=\"6750\" height=\"9000\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=6750&amp;h=9000&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" alt=\"The Milky Way galaxy rises from the horizon and curves upward in the sky. In the foreground, a rock pathway is seen that culminates in a staircase that ends abruptly. The Milky Way band appears to be a continuation of the staircase. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\" decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=6750&amp;h=9000&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 6750w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=225&amp;h=300&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 225w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=768&amp;h=1024&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=1152&amp;h=1536&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1152w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=1536&amp;h=2048&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1536w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=300&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 300w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=450&amp;h=600&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 450w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=675&amp;h=900&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 675w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=900&amp;h=1200&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 900w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_6750.jpg?w=1500&amp;h=2000&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 6750px) 100vw, 6750px\"><img width=\"960\" height=\"1280\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=960&amp;h=1280&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" alt=\"The Milky Way galaxy rises from the horizon and curves upward in the sky. In the foreground, a rock pathway is seen that culminates in a staircase that ends abruptly. The Milky Way band appears to be a continuation of the staircase. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=960&amp;h=1280&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 960w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=225&amp;h=300&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 225w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=768&amp;h=1024&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=300&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 300w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=450&amp;h=600&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 450w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=675&amp;h=900&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 675w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Stairway2Heaven_Looten_960_annotated.jpg?w=900&amp;h=1200&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 900w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 960px) 100vw, 960px\"></p></div> <div> <p><strong>Explanation: </strong>This stairway in Chile brings you only a little bit closer to heaven. But ooh, the sky above it <a href=\"https://youtu.be/Ly6ZhQVnVow\">makes you wonder</a>. For example, you may wonder about the dark lane of our own <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way galaxy</a> rising behind the expansive <a href=\"https://youtu.be/o5JfmFSBDgE\">Atacama Desert</a> in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chile\">Chile</a>, the band of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-september-11-zodiacal-light-before-dawn/\">zodiacal light</a> on the upper left, the green and red <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-february-3-an-airglow-fan-from-lake-to-sky/\">airglow</a> just above the horizon, the star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spica\">Spica</a> in the zodiacal light stripe, and, at the very top on the right, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-september-3-the-small-cloud-of-magellan/\">Small Magellanic Cloud</a> galaxy. The Desert Staircase sculpture is in the foreground, and this image was timed to catch the picturesque peering perch just in front of the near-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-august-15-perseid-rain/\">vertical Milky Way band</a>. Upon <a href=\"https://static.boredpanda.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/funny-dog-nose-closeups-30__700.jpg\">close inspection</a> of the horizon you can find, left to right, some <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andes\">Andes</a> Mountains, the <a href=\"https://youtu.be/-snTWaT1RVk\">Licancabur volcano</a>, and the town lights of <a href=\"https://youtu.be/3xvnQitpPeU\">San Pedro de Atacama</a>. The <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/julienlooten/55575540456/\">featured wide-angle composite</a> was captured in July 2025.</p><p><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>open space </p></div> </div></div>",
